th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028+v3-2026-11-02+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+type-mix-retire-20261112+type-mix-topup-20261112+type-mix-retire2-20261112+fill-20261112+fill2-20261112","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Programming-for-EOR/atomic-vector.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Programming-for-EOR/atomic-vector.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement about the object created by `x <- 5` is correct?","options":["It is a scalar, a separate kind of object, so `length(x)` gives an error.","It becomes a vector only through `c(5)`; without `c()` it has no length.","It is a vector of length 1, so `length(x)` is 1 and `x[1]` is 5.","It is a vector of length 1, but positions start at 0, so `x[0]` is 5."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e6efbcb4a0d6eec4432c9713","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A student wants one object that keeps `c(1, 2)` and `c(3, 4, 5)` as two separate pieces and runs `z <- c(c(1, 2), c(3, 4, 5))`. What does `z` hold?","options":["One flat vector of length 5; `c()` never nests, so separate pieces need a list.","Two elements, each a vector, since `c()` keeps its arguments as they are.","Nothing: `c()` cannot combine vectors of lengths 2 and 3 and stops with an error.","A vector of length 6, after `c(1, 2)` is recycled to the length 3 of the other."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"964e96ad7c8d840000b08b49","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In linear algebra $(1, 2, 3)$ is a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^3$. Which statement compares the R vector `x <- c(1, 2, 3)` with it correctly?","options":["`x` is a column vector with 3 rows and 1 column, as in linear algebra.","The elements of `x` are numbered 0, 1 and 2 instead of 1, 2 and 3.","`x` must hold numbers; a vector of text values is not possible in R.","`x` has no row or column orientation; it is an ordered sequence of 3 numbers."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a2cde8ae969c221d6ea1c885","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does `length(c(\"data analysis\", \"R\"))` return?","options":["`[1] 14`","`[1] 2`","`[1] 3`","`[1] 1`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c28ed4834059a9067ae205fd","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print after `a <- c(7, 1); b <- 3; c(b, a)`?","options":["`[1] 7 1 3`","`[1] 3 7 1`","`[1] 1 3 7`","`[1] 10  4`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"aa6e823d0d17477cbb11f602","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print for `vector(\"character\", 2)`?","options":["`[1] NA NA`","`[1] \"character\" \"character\"`","`[1] \"\" \"\"`","`[1] \" \" \" \"`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3ce62b3769e8087046033e91","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A character vector is typed with single quotes, `v <- c('A', 'B')`. What does R print for `v`?","options":["`[1] \"A\" \"B\"`","`[1] 'A' 'B'`","`[1] A B`","`[1] \"A\", \"B\"`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0686d1806a805008ad5f3074","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which call creates a numeric vector of five zeros?","options":["`vector(5, \"numeric\")`","`c(0, 5)`","`vector(\"logical\", 5)`","`vector(\"numeric\", 5)`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0ca3dab54bc0610a277ae21e","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does `x <- c(4, 5); y <- c(x, 6); c(length(x), length(y))` return?","options":["`[1] 3 3`","`[1] 2 2`","`[1] 2 3`","`[1] 0 3`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"46de1bd1b524bec4eabc4eeb","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does `length(matrix(0, nrow = 2, ncol = 3))` return?","options":["`[1] 2`","`[1] 6`","`[1] 3`","`[1] 2 3`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8fef77b5da802ff23b607c18","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which of these objects is not an atomic vector?","options":["`c(1, \"a\")`","`5`","`\"hello\"`","`list(1, \"a\")`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5b99de9f92992b93ffbb6e4d","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement correctly compares the vectors `c(3)` and `vector(\"numeric\", 3)`?","options":["`c(3)` has one element, the number 3; `vector(\"numeric\", 3)` holds three zeros.","Both are the single number 3; `vector()` only states that it is numeric.","Both have three elements, since the 3 sets the length in each call.","`c(3)` is a scalar rather than a vector; only `vector(\"numeric\", 3)` is a vector."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e96ab0cd62a0dbe9066a31b3","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"In linear algebra the vectors $(1,2,3)$ and $(4,5,6)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ have the sum $(5,7,9)$. With `u <- c(1, 2, 3)` and `v <- c(4, 5, 6)`, which call returns `[1] 5 7 9`?","options":["`c(u, v)`","`sum(u, v)`","`u + v`","`c(sum(u), sum(v))`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"771662f53e6f92816c8643aa","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print after `v <- vector(\"numeric\", 3); v[2] <- 8; v`?","options":["`[1] NA  8 NA`","`[1] 0 8 0`","`[1] 0 0 8`","`[1] 8`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"31a7ec2b69808ea592bb2ee6","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print after `a <- c(2, 4); b <- c(a, a); a <- 10; b`?","options":["`[1] 2 4 2 4`","`[1] 10 10`","`[1] 10  4 10  4`","`[1]  2  4 10`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2d20e7132b44314ec738ab6a","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What does `c(\"a\", c(\"b\", \"c\"))[2]` return?","options":["`[1] \"b\" \"c\"`","`[1] \"c\"`","`[1] \"a\" \"b\"`","`[1] \"b\"`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9a166e528eaf02a543cd632d","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For any two atomic vectors `x` and `y` of the same type, which statement about `z <- c(x, y)` always holds?","options":["`length(z)` equals the larger of the two lengths, since the shorter one is recycled.","`length(z)` equals `length(x) + length(y)`, with the values of `x` first.","`z` contains each value only once, since `c()` drops repeated values.","`length(z)` is 2, since `c()` keeps each argument as one element."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"aa39c8ccc724bdc375ee2094","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print for `c(vector(\"numeric\", 2), vector(\"logical\", 1))`?","options":["`[1] 0 0 0`","`[1]  0  0 NA`","`[1] \"0\"     \"0\"     \"FALSE\"`","`[1] 0 0`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"290b1190a7ddfdf386331242","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"`e <- vector(\"numeric\", 0)` creates a numeric vector of length 0. What does R print for `c(e, 4, e)`?","options":["`[1] 0 4 0`","`[1] NA  4 NA`","`[1] 4`","`numeric(0)`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3791b08110c479d446dd17a5","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_atomic_vector_5abefcf5_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"What does `length(c(\"\", vector(\"character\", 2)))` return?","options":["`[1] 0`","`[1] 2`","`[1] 1`","`[1] 3`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d8721b082419d8c20abba850","time_limit":40}]}}